  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free and mild conditions will persist \nthrough the end of October\n\nNoon Update...\nForecast on track. No tweaks worth noting to the next 24 hrs.\n\nPrevious discussion...\nIn the wake of a moisture-deprived cold front last night, we'll\nsee another dry day across the Commonwealth with very low \ndewpoints in northeast PA. Temperatures will be cooler than \nMonday, but still several degrees above normal for this time of \nyear. The cold front will lift north again as a warm front on \nWednesday, bringing increasing clouds but no rain chances. \n\nTemperatures will trend warmer through Friday ahead of another\ncold front and then decrease a bit into the weekend. Saturday\nwill be similar to Wednesday with increasing clouds but no rain.\nDaytime and overnight temperatures will be +5 to +15 degrees\nabove average for this time of year through the weekend,\nproviding great conditions for trick or treat. Whether\nneighborhoods are hosting beggars night on Halloween or moving\nit earlier, mild and dry conditions will prevail. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending wetter to start November\n\nAfter not seeing a drop of rain in nearly 2 weeks, November will\nstart off with a couple chances for rain. There remains some\nmodel uncertainty, but a trough should move through the central\npart of the United States early next week. This should deliver\nenough upper level support to generate some accumulating\nrainfall across the Commonwealth. Climate Prediction Center\noutlooks lean wetter than average for the first couple of weeks\nin November, though the signal is not super strong. Temperature\ntrends favor above normal temperatures with much higher\nconfidence. Notably, the monthly outlook for November begins to\ntake on a classic El Nino pattern with above normal \nprecipitation favored across the southeast US up through the \nI-95 corridor and below normal precipitation for the Great \nLakes. This could set the stage for an active storm track\nbringing wet conditions to the Lower Susquehanna Valley while\nthe northwest mountains continue their dry trend.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Fire weather concerns may start to increase later\nthis week.\n\nMin RH values will be lowest this afternoon (mid 30s N to m50s S)\nand rise for Wed-Thur as dew points increase thanks to return\nflow as the high pressure overhead moves to the east. The winds\nwill pick up a bit for the same time frame and for Friday, too.\nThe RHs will likely drop some Friday and even further on Sat\nafter a cold front moves through on Friday. This reduction in\nRH and increase in wind speeds could increase the risk of rapid\nwildfire spread, especially when combined with any reduction in\nfuel moisture. Collaboration on Fire Weather issues may be \nnecessary, at the least worthwhile, toward week's end.",
